from tn import *
def C(ch,*a,**k): TN(9,"science",ch,*a,**k)
C("periodic-classification-of-elements","Asha arranged her class books by subject and size. \"Elements were arranged in a table in the same way,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Early scientists tried to arrange elements in order. Dobereiner grouped them in triads, and Mendeleev arranged them by atomic mass and left gaps for elements not yet found. He even predicted the properties of germanium, which later matched very closely when it was discovered.",
  "The Modern Periodic Law states that the properties of elements are periodic functions of their atomic numbers. In the modern table the elements are arranged in increasing order of atomic number. The horizontal rows are periods and the vertical columns are groups.",
  "The table has s, p, d and f blocks. Elements of group 2 are alkaline earth metals, the d block elements are transition elements, and the p block holds metals, non-metals and metalloids. Hydrogen is the lightest and first element of the table."],
 ["Dobereiner's triads","Mendeleev","modern periodic law","period","group","s p d f blocks"],
 ("In which block is an element with outer configuration ending in s2 placed?",["Elements whose last electrons enter the s sub-shell form the s block.","Group 2 elements end in s2.","So it is in the s block."]),
 ("The modern table is arranged by atomic mass.",["Mendeleev used atomic mass.","The modern table uses atomic number."]),
 ["Mendeleev arranged by atomic mass and left gaps.","The modern table is arranged by atomic number.","Rows are periods and columns are groups.","The table has s, p, d and f blocks."],
 ["m|The horizontal rows of the table are called|periods|groups|blocks|Periods run across.","t|Mendeleev left gaps for undiscovered elements.|T|Yes.","m|The modern periodic table is arranged by increasing|atomic number|atomic mass|density|Modern periodic law.","t|Vertical columns are called periods.|F|They are called groups.","m|Group 2 elements are|alkaline earth metals|noble gases|halogens|Beryllium, magnesium and others.","s|Which element is first in the periodic table?|hydrogen|It is the lightest.","m|Transition elements belong to the|d block|s block|p block|Their properties lie between s and p blocks.","t|Dobereiner grouped elements in triads.|T|Yes."])
C("chemical-bonding","Raju noticed that table salt dissolves in water but sugar does not conduct electricity. \"Their bonds must be different,\" he said.",
 ["Atoms combine to form molecules and the attractive force that holds them is a chemical bond. Kossel and Lewis explained this in 1916 by the octet rule: atoms combine to gain the stable electronic configuration of a noble gas, usually eight electrons in the outer shell.",
  "In an ionic or electrovalent bond one atom gives electrons and another takes them, forming positive and negative ions held by electrostatic attraction. The number of electrons lost or gained is the electrovalency. In magnesium chloride, magnesium has 2, 8, 2 and loses two electrons while each chlorine with 2, 8, 7 gains one.",
  "In a covalent bond atoms share electrons. Lewis dot structures show the outer electrons as dots. The kind of bond decides the properties of the compound. Oxidation is loss of electrons and reduction is gain of electrons."],
 ["octet rule","ionic bond","electrovalency","covalent bond","Lewis dot structure","oxidation and reduction"],
 ("Sodium (2, 8, 1) and chlorine (2, 8, 7) combine. What happens?",["Sodium loses its one outer electron.","Chlorine gains that electron.","Na+ and Cl- attract to form the ionic bond in NaCl."]),
 ("In an ionic bond electrons are shared equally.",["Ionic bonds involve transfer of electrons.","Sharing happens in covalent bonds."]),
 ["Atoms bond to get a noble gas arrangement.","Ionic bond: transfer; covalent bond: sharing.","Oxidation is loss, reduction is gain of electrons.","Bond type decides properties."],
 ["m|The octet rule says atoms try to have|eight outer electrons|two protons|ten neutrons|Like noble gases.","t|An ionic bond forms by transfer of electrons.|T|Yes.","m|A bond formed by sharing electrons is|covalent|ionic|metallic only|Atoms share pairs.","t|Oxidation is gain of electrons.|F|It is loss of electrons.","m|Electrostatic attraction holds|ions together|neutrons together|molecules to air|Positive and negative ions.","s|Name the bond in NaCl.|ionic;electrovalent|Ionic or electrovalent.","m|Dots showing outer electrons make a|Lewis dot structure|periodic table|graph|Shown around the symbol.","t|Magnesium loses two electrons in magnesium chloride.|T|2, 8, 2 to 2, 8."])
C("acids-bases-and-salts","Meena squeezed lemon on her turmeric-stained cloth and the colour changed. \"Acids change colours,\" she said.",
 ["Substances with a sour taste are acids, and bases are substances that ionise in water to give hydroxide ions (OH-) according to Arrhenius. Bases soluble in water are alkalis. Acids are classed by basicity as monobasic, dibasic and tribasic according to the replaceable hydrogen ions per molecule.",
  "Indicators such as litmus show acids and bases by colour change. When an acid and a base react a neutral product, a salt, forms together with water, and this is a neutralisation reaction, for example HCl + NaOH gives NaCl + H2O. Sulphuric acid is called the king of chemicals because many compounds are made from it.",
  "Salts are classed as normal, acidic and basic, and some crystallise with water of crystallisation, as in copper sulphate. The strength of an acid or base is measured on the pH scale, and salts can be identified by simple tests."],
 ["acid","base and alkali","indicator","neutralisation","salt","pH scale"],
 ("Complete: HCl + NaOH gives ___ and water.",["An acid and a base react to give a salt and water.","The salt here is sodium chloride.","HCl + NaOH gives NaCl + H2O."]),
 ("All bases dissolve in water.",["Only some bases are soluble.","Those that dissolve are called alkalis."]),
 ["Acids are sour; bases ionise to give OH-.","Acid + base gives salt + water.","Indicators show acids and bases by colour.","pH measures strength."],
 ["m|The reaction of an acid with a base is|neutralisation|combustion|distillation|It gives salt and water.","t|Sulphuric acid is called king of chemicals.|T|Yes.","m|A base soluble in water is an|alkali|salt|indicator|Like NaOH.","t|Acids taste bitter.|F|They taste sour.","m|Litmus is an|indicator|acid|salt|It changes colour.","s|What is formed besides water in neutralisation?|salt|A salt.","m|Acid with one replaceable hydrogen is|monobasic|dibasic|tribasic|One H+ per molecule.","t|Blue vitriol has water of crystallisation.|T|Copper sulphate crystals contain water."])
C("carbon-and-its-compounds","Raju held a diamond ring and a pencil lead. \"Both are carbon!\" said his teacher, and he could hardly believe it.",
 ["Carbon is the main constituent of coal and forms two classes of compounds: organic and inorganic. It has special features. Catenation is the ability of carbon atoms to join in long chains and rings, and tetravalency means carbon has four bonds. Carbon can form single, double and triple bonds, which is why there are so many carbon compounds.",
  "Isomerism is the phenomenon in which the same molecular formula has different structural arrangements, and such compounds are isomers; C2H6O is an example. Allotropy is the existence of an element in different forms: diamond, graphite and fullerene are crystalline forms of carbon while charcoal and coke are amorphous.",
  "Plastics are catenated carbon compounds. They are useful but harm the environment because they do not decay. New rules ban some single-use plastic items, and students can help by refusing, reusing and recycling plastic in daily life."],
 ["catenation","tetravalency","isomerism","allotropy","diamond and graphite","plastics"],
 ("Name two crystalline forms of carbon.",["Crystalline forms have regular arrangements of atoms.","Diamond and graphite are two examples.","Fullerene is also one."]),
 ("Diamond and graphite are different elements.",["Both are made only of carbon.","They are allotropes with different atom arrangements."]),
 ["Carbon has catenation and tetravalency.","Isomers share a formula but differ in structure.","Allotropes are different forms of one element.","Reduce plastic use."],
 ["m|Joining of carbon atoms in chains is|catenation|isomerism|allotropy|A special property of carbon.","t|Carbon has a valency of four.|T|Tetravalency.","m|Diamond and graphite are|allotropes|isomers|isotopes|Forms of carbon.","t|Charcoal is a crystalline form of carbon.|F|It is amorphous.","m|Same molecular formula but different structure is|isomerism|catenation|ionisation|Isomers.","s|Name the carbon form used in pencils.|graphite|Graphite is soft.","m|A harm of plastics is that they|do not easily decay|dissolve in water|are very rare|They pollute land.","t|Fullerene is an allotrope of carbon.|T|Yes."])
